The CI job for this system, WAITING TO BE APPLIED BY A MAINTAINER.

    git apply test/system/rb-solar/ci-job.patch
    git commit -am 'CI: run the rb-solar system job'

It is a patch here rather than a job in .github/workflows/build.yml
because the session that wrote rb-solar cannot push workflow files:
GitHub refuses an OAuth app without the `workflow` scope, with

  ! [remote rejected] ... (refusing to allow an OAuth App to create
  or update workflow '.github/workflows/build.yml' without 'workflow'
  scope)

A patch rather than a paste-this block, because a patch is checkable:
`git apply --check` says whether it still fits the workflow file, and
this one is regenerated whenever that file moves. Once applied, delete
it -- nothing else in the repository refers to it.

git apply ignores everything above the first `diff --git` line, so this
note travels with the change it describes.

diff --git a/.github/workflows/build.yml b/.github/workflows/build.yml
index 7eb1c1bb..9e61a8b4 100644
--- a/.github/workflows/build.yml
+++ b/.github/workflows/build.yml
@@ -162,3 +162,64 @@ jobs:
         working-directory: ts
       - run: ./run-all.sh
         working-directory: use-cases
+
+  # THE SYSTEM THAT BOOTS.
+  #
+  # `use-cases/run-all.sh` above already reaches test/system/rb-solar,
+  # and on a runner with no Ruby bundle it runs the seven checks that do
+  # not need one: the generators, the tree they must reproduce, the
+  # diagrams, the model's coverage and its form. The three it skips are
+  # the ones that make the system a system -- the app boots, the
+  # reference's own twenty validation tests pass against it over HTTP,
+  # its Ruby SDK drives it, and the human pages answer.
+  #
+  # Those need Ruby, a bundle and the reference checkout, which is a
+  # different shape of job from the use-case one and would slow it for
+  # every model it drives. Hence a job of its own; check.sh is the same
+  # script either way and reports which legs it ran.
+  system-rb-solar:
+    name: System (rb-solar)
+    timeout-minutes: 20
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+
+      # NODE 24 IS NOT INCIDENTAL. The reference's validate.ts is
+      # TypeScript run by node directly; type stripping arrives in
+      # 22.18, and check.sh skips that leg with the version in the note
+      # on anything older rather than dying in the parser.
+      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
+        with:
+          node-version: 24.x
+
+      - uses: ruby/setup-ruby@v1
+        with:
+          ruby-version: '3.3'
+          bundler-cache: true
+          working-directory: test/system/rb-solar/app
+
+      # The engine the check drives: $AONTU defaults to ts/bin/aontu.js,
+      # which loads the committed ts/dist. Build for the same reason the
+      # use-case job does -- an unbuilt tree grades this branch's model
+      # against the previous branch's engine.
+      - run: npm install
+        working-directory: ts
+      - run: npm run build
+        working-directory: ts
+
+      # The reference repository, for the SDK leg only. Public, shallow,
+      # and read once: ref/ already carries the OpenAPI description, the
+      # validation script and the seed data at a pinned commit. Without
+      # this clone that one leg skips with a note and the other nine
+      # still run.
+      - name: Fetch the reference SDK
+        run: |
+          git clone --depth 1 \
+            https://github.com/voxgig-sdk/voxgig-solardemo-sdk.git \
+            "$RUNNER_TEMP/solardemo"
+
+      - name: rb-solar
+        working-directory: test/system/rb-solar
+        env:
+          RB_SOLAR_SDK: ${{ runner.temp }}/solardemo/rb
+        run: ./check.sh
